From 5e2d22ba640ecf952b23ed50c87c01835ddfcb8b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lane-coordinator <agent@creation-guidelines>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 20:15:34 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 2/6] watchdog: pm8916_wdt: Stop the watchdog on reboot and
 unregister

The PMIC watchdog's S2 stage outlives a reset of the SoC: its enable
bit and timers stay set across a reboot. Rebooted with the watchdog
open, the next kernel finds it running, and a kernel without this
driver is reset every timeout seconds (seen on a PM6150: a reboot loop
that only a full power-off ended).

Stop it on an orderly reboot or shutdown and when the device goes away.
A bite still leaves S2 enabled; the next kernel's driver finds it
running and keeps petting it until userspace takes over.

Written with an AI coding assistant (Claude); reviewed and tested as
described in the cover letter.
---
 drivers/watchdog/pm8916_wdt.c | 7 +++++++
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/watchdog/pm8916_wdt.c b/drivers/watchdog/pm8916_wdt.c
index c221c4adb..11b40e0d1 100644
--- a/drivers/watchdog/pm8916_wdt.c
+++ b/drivers/watchdog/pm8916_wdt.c
@@ -280,6 +280,13 @@ static int pm8916_wdt_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 	watchdog_init_timeout(&wdt->wdev, 0, dev);
 	pm8916_wdt_configure_timers(&wdt->wdev);
 
+	/*
+	 * S2 outlives a reset of the SoC: left enabled, it bites the next
+	 * kernel, and one without this driver resets every timeout seconds.
+	 */
+	watchdog_stop_on_reboot(&wdt->wdev);
+	watchdog_stop_on_unregister(&wdt->wdev);
+
 	return devm_watchdog_register_device(dev, &wdt->wdev);
 }
 
